WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.733
A2C.

2
00:00:02.033 --> 00:00:06.667
Simple, synchronous, and the cleanest way to see exactly what PPO adds.

3
00:00:07.867 --> 00:00:11.267
A2C stands for advantage actor-critic, synchronous.

4
00:00:11.567 --> 00:00:17.000
Several environment copies step in lockstep, and their experience is pooled into one batch.

5
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:19.633
Here is one iteration.

6
00:00:19.933 --> 00:00:22.133
All the environments take a step together.

7
00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:24.467
Their experience becomes one batch.

8
00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:29.725
The actor updates along the advantage with a single, unclipped gradient step.

9
00:00:29.725 --> 00:00:30.498
Then repeat.

10
00:00:30.498 --> 00:00:33.267
Nothing protects against an oversized step.

11
00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:36.000
So what does PPO add?

12
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.967
Both are actor-critic with parallel environments.

13
00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:41.300
A2C takes one unclipped step.

14
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:46.033
PPO clips its updates, and reuses each batch for multiple epochs.

15
00:00:46.333 --> 00:00:48.633
That one-line formula is the difference.

16
00:00:49.833 --> 00:00:53.433
Run both on the same environment, same budget, same seed.

17
00:00:53.733 --> 00:01:00.000
A2C typically reaches about three eighty, with wobble, because a bad batch can knock the policy over.

18
00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:02.224
PPO sits at five hundred, flat.

19
00:01:02.224 --> 00:01:03.900
The difference is the clip.

20
00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:06.833
Here's the comparison as code.

21
00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:08.867
We loop over both algorithms.

22
00:01:09.167 --> 00:01:10.800
Same seed, same budget.

23
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:14.933
And we evaluate each over twenty episodes, printing mean and spread.

24
00:01:16.133 --> 00:01:18.733
Say you propose PPO for the cubesat sim.

25
00:01:19.033 --> 00:01:21.433
A good reviewer asks: compared to what?

26
00:01:21.733 --> 00:01:26.616
The A2C run is your answer, same budget and seeds, logged side by side.

27
00:01:26.616 --> 00:01:32.600
It protects you from a fancy algorithm winning only because nobody ran the simpler one.

28
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:34.641
Let's recap.

29
00:01:34.641 --> 00:01:37.933
A2C is actor-critic with parallel environments.

30
00:01:38.233 --> 00:01:40.333
It takes one unclipped step per batch.

31
00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:44.300
PPO is A2C plus clipping and multiple epochs.

32
00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.225
And always run the simple baseline.

33
00:01:47.225 --> 00:01:48.200
Next up: A3C.
